---
name: dsh-runtime-guide
description: Use when an agent will change, restart, hot-reload, upgrade, or debug a running DeepSeek Harness host, or when plugin work could break the current session, profile, or host. Read-only diagnosis first; no global install from inside a session.
---

# DSH runtime guide

Load `model-policy` once from its host catalog location and apply its guidance.

The agent may be running **inside the host it is about to change**. Treat that as a
safety boundary, not as a detail.

## Sandbox boundaries

A DSH command runs in a **file-effect** sandbox, not a VM. On Linux bwrap the process gets
a private `/dev` and PID namespace, an ephemeral `/tmp`, and only the workspace/extra
roots writable; network, host processes and GPU devices are outside the policy. Therefore
`nvidia-smi` failing or `/dev/nvidia*` being absent in the command is **not proof the host
has no GPU**. Check host evidence (`/sys/class/drm`, `/proc/driver/nvidia/version`), state
the boundary, and request the narrowest wider mode or a host runner for hardware work.
Never turn a sandbox limitation into a plugin/runtime finding. Details:
[sandbox boundaries](references/sandbox-boundaries.md).

## First check

1. Identify the running process, its `DSH_HOME`, profile, port, and argv.
2. Compare the process start with the checkout's last commit and the running entry's
   mtime; a dirty tree or an entry changed after start weakens or proves a ghost.
3. Decide whether the change is client-only, host/plugin, profile composition, or
   the dsh package itself.

Run `node scripts/inspect-host.mjs --pid <dsh-host-pid> --checkout <path>` (read-only)
when the process is reachable. The PID is required; do not infer it from the shell. If
the process is the current session, state that explicitly.


## Upgrade request protocol

When the request is to upgrade or restart the **current** dsh host, do not attempt it from
inside the session. Do not run a package-manager global install, do not request
`danger-full-access`, and do not run `kill`/`systemctl restart`. First answer with the
external runbook and why the in-session path is unsafe. Only proceed after the user
confirms they will run the command outside this host. This applies even when the prompt
says "upgrade now".

## Change impact

| Change | Safe action |
|---|---|
| Client asset only | Build, refresh/HMR only while `pnpm run dev:web` is running; otherwise rebuild and hard-refresh. Host restart only if the client registration changed |
| Host plugin code or row | Build, restart the host under its supervisor, then verify boot and one real path |
| Profile bundle/dependency/patch | Plan the profile edit, confirm, then restart the host; never rewrite the user's patch |
| dsh package itself | **Never run a global install from inside a dsh session.** Stop the dsh processes that own this install/prefix (not unrelated hosts), then run a pinned install from an external shell |
| Running host is the target | Do not explain its behavior with the new disk version; restart it or pin analysis to its actual generation |

## Hard boundaries

- Do not run `npm install -g @deepseek-ai/dsh@...`, `npm update -g`, or a package-manager
  upgrade of the live host from inside a session that is executing from that package
  tree. The installation can replace the running files, kill the session, and leave
  missing shims and a broken `dsh` command.
- Do not overwrite another agent's or the user's profile patch, lockfile, or working
  tree to "make it work."
- Identify processes by their install prefix/profile/port before stopping them; do not kill unrelated hosts.

- Do not run unbounded heavy builds, tests, or subagents in the live host. Use a
  bounded job, a separate profile/port, or a throwaway checkout; watch memory,
  process count, and session growth.
- Do not report success from an exit code alone. Verify the profile composition, boot,
  the served client, and one real path.

## Recovery

EBUSY is a lock/atomic-replace error, not proof of a native module. dsh's session JSONL
lease and Windows atomic replace can both produce it; identify the lock owner and stop
only the host/profile that owns it before replacing files. A missing `dsh` command after an interrupted global install needs an
external pinned reinstall, not hand-copied package directories. A ghost host needs a
restart before its behavior can be compared with the disk. See
[upgrade recovery](references/upgrade-recovery.md) for the exact sequence.

## Verify

Run the host's own version/profile/boot checks, then one message → tool → response
path for a host change, or a real client fetch/mount for a client change. Record what
you did not test. Read [host lifecycle](references/host-lifecycle.md),
[upgrade recovery](references/upgrade-recovery.md), and
[performance limits](references/performance.md) only when the affected boundary needs
them.

